  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2)$ are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/65a899ed4375be4681c0e4598cc4769335cabb52257a886148438f4b4f16e671.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$",
